      {
        "id": "book-spine",
        "relation": "evaluation_comparison",
        "reason": "두 연구 모두 한국어 OCR 전사 성능을 CER로 평가해 문자 오류율 산정 방식과 실제 OCR 적용 결과를 함께 살펴볼 수 있습니다.",
        "sourceEvidence": {
          "articleId": "kodistill",
          "blockId": "[\"korean-ocr-a-measurable-change\",3]",
          "anchor": "korean-ocr-a-measurable-change",
          "quote": "CER drops substantially, but it is not interchangeable with word accuracy. Overlong or repeated wrong transcriptions can add many character insertions. The base, V2, and final K-STR outputs have 24, 22, and one truncated generations respectively, all retained in scoring. Better answer length and fewer runaway outputs can contribute alongside better text recognition; we do not attribute the CER reduction solely to glyph recognition.",
          "hash": "b680323399afa0ff3a95ac72e8ade2bdd8ff7053a50110208f640b5ab3b1c1a6"
        },
        "targetEvidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"metrics\",5]",
          "anchor": "metrics",
          "quote": "CER (character error rate) is the edit distance between the title line and the true title, both with spaces and punctuation removed, divided by the length of the true title and averaged over spines; each Hangul syllable counts as one character.",
          "hash": "de949fece572e50a28e22f960745ba22b7e8dfcbc381628f2016a633ddab42ec"
        }
      },
